b/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247788} {:answer/id #uuid "43c564eb-1cec-5068-bb46-c7d448a4689f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248029} {:answer/id #uuid "60377ceb-0560-5dd4-89d1-60433d96dd2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248294} {:answer/id #uuid "b61c8aaa-158b-52aa-930a-c5c6b5128ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249053} {:answer/id #uuid "ff97869d-be3b-5c18-bc98-f3b9c2a66095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b61c8aaa-158b-52aa-930a-c5c6b5128ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249053} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f70073-768b-5c21-9eb3-fe3ffbc2f0bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d48a1800-0baf-5dcd-a800-d0ee3d7aed53" :question/math-academy-id "q-115435" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,-1),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=-1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff86e-cbd2-5244-bd7e-fa7501ce1356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247533} {:answer/id #uuid "6d01c7a7-ed12-581a-857f-30f616b9ef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248388} {:answer/id #uuid "711453e3-73f9-5722-820f-ac643ba5fcb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248413} {:answer/id #uuid "8d999a07-30ce-573b-8c2d-fa8220991104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=4(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248686} {:answer/id #uuid "d6aed572-9d95-5182-8bd1-9ea1faa87eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff86e-cbd2-5244-bd7e-fa7501ce1356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247533} :answer-field/id #uuid "242c488e-b1d2-5f9b-924c-0c83035c9cef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af2d74a7-36e3-5f6f-95f7-10b5d7e5decf" :question/math-academy-id "q-115451" :question/problem "![](images/97/970db64a0ddd325993323511ad4ae754ce64f64d591877bfa0ac908874e2393f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-1).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(3-1) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =2(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e5e768-df1f-5d74-ae7a-7fc38729e30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{16}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247488} {:answer/id #uuid "12140e6e-87e7-5362-8ef1-3152c113ebd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247586} {:answer/id #uuid "27a91fe5-ec69-5a55-89f5-4cbe9dbddc03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247777} {:answer/id #uuid "96f198f9-c262-5f1b-bcbf-33228ef0638e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248771} {:answer/id #uuid "d06f6c37-a466-50b0-b008-678db1636504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12140e6e-87e7-5362-8ef1-3152c113ebd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247586} :answer-field/id #uuid "43ab0a42-397a-53a0-87b7-b26848ed22d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af0b97a8-b635-5432-9208-09a4d6d1e1c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115500" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e26f3115e64d22d336c164fca762a1654ce515019f425f5b0b772276ca88f727.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,4),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (1-4)^{2} & =4p(4) \\\\ 9 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{16})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f8ad212-90af-5030-91e9-e77eed90aa90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{4}{28}(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247853} {:answer/id #uuid "632c0ad0-e7ff-5d95-acad-0af5067a4924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248324} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcf9f6e-2849-59c7-bb97-59ce391b7323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=3(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248528} {:answer/id #uuid "a03b5184-19c0-5cca-be72-38a2008cfc4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=3(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248853} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d43b64-b98e-5b9d-8951-94466d0a2d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{28}(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a03b5184-19c0-5cca-be72-38a2008cfc4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=3(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248853} :answer-field/id #uuid "27d97fb2-5838-5cec-9d00-faace91d5216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "853be803-536b-569b-a3cc-9828143249b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115506" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,-5)$ and which passes through the point $(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-1,-5).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-1))^{2} & =4p(y-(-5)) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(2,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (2+1)^{2} & =4p(-2+5) \\\\ 9 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})(y+5) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =3(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025ef3a4-70ce-52be-b6d0-841afe01115f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247440} {:answer/id #uuid "a86c5836-b30c-52df-9e07-609b42fb6c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248920} {:answer/id #uuid "c2584ac3-e62f-5e7f-87a1-66f103ed801d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249150} {:answer/id #uuid "d5855c13-f8e9-5c30-922d-3b629e92e2e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=5(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249316} {:answer/id #uuid "d72093e1-3f29-5cf1-8fa0-11aa9afee3ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a86c5836-b30c-52df-9e07-609b42fb6c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248920} :answer-field/id #uuid "6336bb4c-e327-501a-9829-12a627d4800c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92e783e4-7f06-5937-98d3-6bf3624a8108" :question/math-academy-id "q-115509" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-3)$ and which passes through the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(1,-3).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-(-3)) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-1,2),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3) \\\\ (-1-1)^{2} & =4p(2+3) \\\\ 4 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{5})(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a986a9-dc99-5796-9957-e44b0b8d9b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247416} {:answer/id #uuid "3af46962-42bf-52d0-9044-0ca1c67c1f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247946} {:answer/id #uuid "40f40afb-bd2f-53ad-bfee-4cedf94cb572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-4(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248000} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a6a65-91c8-5dc8-8832-b63911bb283a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249388} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0b7d8e-f5b6-5077-a852-7535675aef4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{16}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a6a65-91c8-5dc8-8832-b63911bb283a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249388} :answer-field/id #uuid "a77272b0-ce4e-560d-9231-c7789014a60c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55c7e4a7-0bdd-5125-954e-1f3a2bdbf235" :question/math-academy-id "q-115513" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(4,0)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (1-4)^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 9 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{9}{16})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =-\\frac{9}{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "142f2f77-a4b7-5b48-bf84-e7dcbd48b69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247605} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddafea7-b413-5ba3-a019-c366151f6773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247831} {:answer/id #uuid "73aab154-b5f4-5037-8868-b6249ce08fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248438} {:answer/id #uuid "79a1dd44-e87e-5396-8592-b303b665b229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248494} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0363c2-ebe9-5343-aca4-ae87280f4870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad0363c2-ebe9-5343-aca4-ae87280f4870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248970} :answer-field/id #uuid "8259a8cd-aeb6-5eb8-84ba-08c5a2aac0b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f96f4a00-e296-51d8-8283-a935ed362b8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115516" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x]^{′}}{{[x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1be917-67fb-578e-954b-abd43a23e348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247974} {:answer/id #uuid "50cbb7df-ff1d-5ec1-b975-423eb45cf6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248148} {:answer/id #uuid "5a30d855-606b-58c6-b70c-741050c6c8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248240} {:answer/id #uuid "c1157ca6-9bce-51d1-a9ee-ceec63429c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249140} {:answer/id #uuid "ce75318e-e092-5621-a3d1-bcdfb26a886b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a30d855-606b-58c6-b70c-741050c6c8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248240} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e8ddb9e-3675-514c-a828-85dac3fe037b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef69b6da-6b79-5e57-a05c-34b900661ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115517" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{x}]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{-{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]^{′}}{[x]^{′}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d36f99a-7b3c-58c4-8e9a-3a21fd763566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247544} {:answer/id #uuid "61a4653f-6df7-51f2-86fa-d13ae68764a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248305} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2148f7-50e6-51e7-9398-cf6ddcd9de74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248668} {:answer/id #uuid "a11b1bc5-2153-59a0-816b-97eff84bc81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248863} {:answer/id #uuid "db8f7095-8449-5145-9fef-5d8ca09214f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d36f99a-7b3c-58c4-8e9a-3a21fd763566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247544} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd93bb03-4956-5c3c-8d6a-9abd4a3f4f9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4bbbb84-0fac-5f07-a8f5-38985b38226e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115518" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}{(\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}){cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b693811-a16b-53ed-9611-68583bdf136e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247672} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4b941c-69b2-577b-a723-cb390aecaad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247825} {:answer/id #uuid "52a8d6c1-c802-52b6-9422-b177b1c39eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248165} {:answer/id #uuid "644a1b11-e2aa-55cd-ba47-d5a074cd81d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248329} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0fcf86-6e40-5292-948f-778a8f8317e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "644a1b11-e2aa-55cd-ba47-d5a074cd81d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248329} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb56519-add1-5a22-b89f-f23b73f9e1db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0b7651b-eacd-5715-8331-dab57a1d4302" :question/math-academy-id "q-115526" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{-{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "442d1826-8ba8-59fe-af61-e804166eea4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248032} {:answer/id #uuid "773d5740-f93f-500c-a1e3-bcf114d1f5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248475} {:answer/id #uuid "83a48ec6-e5bf-5565-8966-159df28981ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248588} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc534d1-7861-539f-acce-b7cca0d2b9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248703} {:answer/id #uuid "e145a029-9b27-5f17-87b3-14bc970c1d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "442d1826-8ba8-59fe-af61-e804166eea4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248032} :answer-field/id #uuid "34b86cf6-fd45-5124-ac9e-a3a0687418ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88fb5994-1227-5032-b75a-adafaeaf4ffe" :question/math-academy-id "q-115528" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x}{(\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d037599-07ed-5ba7-83e9-f8c1efa482f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247821} {:answer/id #uuid "31ae4547-e21a-58af-ba30-0bdc4c6faf8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247874} {:answer/id #uuid "88431084-d59b-514f-a06a-1a663f1974b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248641} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfa8ec-c233-56f4-9f06-28d24d2fad3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249296} {:answer/id #uuid "f67649d3-0d90-50b6-8568-82074b23672e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfa8ec-c233-56f4-9f06-28d24d2fad3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249296} :answer-field/id #uuid "38f9bf5f-e290-5c3d-ab0c-661753693c04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e116ffff-24eb-5f64-952b-b5d8dd36f71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115530" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt{x}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{ln}^{2}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{(\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{8\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f6ccd60-a3e4-5ce6-9d5e-a376f267d343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248406} {:answer/id #uuid "71f7119c-74ea-52d7-a308-7659883775e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248418} {:answer/id #uuid "84779d56-58c2-555d-a3cc-90c133ddaec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248598} {:answer/id #uuid "b961e3af-c091-5872-9218-c014fd5deaff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249081} {:answer/id #uuid "fc333b6b-03c6-59c2-97cd-44b7a727e392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84779d56-58c2-555d-a3cc-90c133ddaec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248598} :answer-field/id #uuid "8264505c-0613-5f02-b2e2-463f0471a6a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4bc1f703-1de2-59b6-981e-831238ea97de" :question/math-academy-id "q-115592" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1)$ and $f(1)={e}^{2}.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{3}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)={e}^{2},$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1),$ then ${f}^{′}(1)=2{e}^{2\\cdot 1}\\cdot 1(1+1)=4{e}^{2}.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{3}{4})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "439853c6-2937-5b91-a8f2-dc8431ada5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248028} {:answer/id #uuid "9034ab44-bfb6-51cc-afa3-631ba91cd9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248705} {:answer/id #uuid "a0de1bab-8f70-530c-869b-42cf67ad5c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248862} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bcb5e0-04c6-5c07-8b21-86f194174368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249317} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d971c4-0509-5ce8-bc45-fbade9865e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "439853c6-2937-5b91-a8f2-dc8431ada5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248028} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8e013a-4dd0-52ac-bae6-4d64d494b145" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e94ede6e-b65e-547c-9e4b-813d5e9b97e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115601" :question/problem "![](images/49/4938d034c766b78e1da1a5bd19f2a12b314f772ca4819cd52d4f1463ff1ae3a4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(3,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(3,2),$ we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(3) \\\\ 4 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{3}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e36eb8-21f0-5f1c-a082-d66656a6790b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247510} {:answer/id #uuid "289823bd-24bc-52a9-a1b5-d43e40baba8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{12}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247785} {:answer/id #uuid "852a3f40-3dd3-50b3-949a-26583a2c107c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248609} {:answer/id #uuid "9a587bd0-68cc-56a2-bdb6-582856b6ff94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248799} {:answer/id #uuid "e730056c-bdc5-54d5-a69b-d38cfd63db08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a587bd0-68cc-56a2-bdb6-582856b6ff94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248799} :answer-field/id #uuid "7665e01f-6bd4-5975-9b08-e87552004f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b404c30-267f-56f0-958b-85883e368d93" :question/math-academy-id "q-115602" :question/problem "![](images/57/5746a438eccdd55d345f1c71a768f971aa1a342798743e151f7557102ddd8783.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(1,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(1,-3),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-3)^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 9 & =4p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{9}{4}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a8cc45c-a34a-53f6-8aef-19e7dde9ab42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248087} {:answer/id #uuid "79364bd6-0177-5c87-baf3-c1208d7a4d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248488} {:answer/id #uuid "8ada4a96-61b9-5028-b037-83a44b2109fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248662} {:answer/id #uuid "e886bd4d-b191-56ad-9f08-468f250218a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249479} {:answer/id #uuid "eea18b97-4f5c-594c-82da-685342d0ffe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79364bd6-0177-5c87-baf3-c1208d7a4d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248488} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddd6cddd-26c0-5b1e-8ffc-6809c2807173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8aa75b6-dda5-58c9-ab6d-95e4d74e59b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115603" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b472d30e6260b0e0f1ce4bc791c5ce3068d78a304711775e1327a4c3dbd8c3d3.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(2,-2),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-2)^{2} & =4p(2) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043d1ce5-9e3e-5519-9a4c-03569c147664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247455} {:answer/id #uuid "554d505f-19eb-54e9-8916-dac052fc21dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248193} {:answer/id #uuid "64b65e65-4617-5e48-b1cf-a0372e6395b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{3}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248333} {:answer/id #uuid "7379ce95-99ed-5b20-9302-b338646b620c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248436} {:answer/id #uuid "d091e770-03db-512b-a3b7-40a0f2053ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "554d505f-19eb-54e9-8916-dac052fc21dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248193} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aca0b8a-ad9d-5b96-a5a6-ae937ea506b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "513d3507-74c5-5ea9-ac09-4c99e11dedbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-115623" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{2x+1}+\\frac{B}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(2x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}=A(2x+1)^{2}+B(2x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =A{(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)}^{2}+B(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2} & =A(2(0)+1)^{2}+B(2(0)+1)+C \\\\ 0 & =A+B+1 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-1)^{2} & =A(2(-1)+1)^{2}+B(2(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 4 & =A-B+1 \\\\ A-B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=3$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =3-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a5a701-4a51-5b68-8e1b-b66e6b850789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247601} {:answer/id #uuid "1f586b44-1122-5f1d-b05e-8923133fd7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247701} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec8b296-2455-5f5c-91c3-439121becad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247843} {:answer/id #uuid "3add8fbd-a7ff-5f7b-a16d-de66af3fe046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247945} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb054cf-6d87-53c2-b259-43caf88b5ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13a5a701-4a51-5b68-8e1b-b66e6b850789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247601} :answer-field/id #uuid "c569f12e-cea8-5587-81d8-e362cff29dac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69e800ff-6717-5a96-b9a0-b74b69055c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115625" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b14a6063e0b1be751e738060087644cacaa3c3cf1e324ee66bf57232834e618d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-1,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,1),$ we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {1}^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5070b1-5b1a-5296-a031-e24091f2b3df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247940} {:answer/id #uuid "59abf281-9efe-56d8-8676-d28dc59a654b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248235} {:answer/id #uuid "716b26ff-dd27-556a-9144-0afa8fa39742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{8}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248414} {:answer/id #uuid "9872c090-a386-58d7-88fa-9f558a1e3b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248784} {:answer/id #uuid "efe6cb33-161c-5882-8edc-73409d4ba88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=12y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59abf281-9efe-56d8-8676-d28dc59a654b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248235} :answer-field/id #uuid "884a7b9a-4071-5cf6-a767-f08c0dbe1f04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77a2fd6b-e9d0-50f6-a357-c099577e8b84" :question/math-academy-id "q-115628" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at  $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(6,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(6,3),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {3}^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 9 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{8})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5d417c-3f72-5468-9639-1f5120b9f091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247522} {:answer/id #uuid "21a8fb1b-59d9-55a8-aee3-2f69f0e83e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247723} {:answer/id #uuid "31a33578-5df7-576f-8acf-c92eae34ca82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247873} {:answer/id #uuid "bd399471-0d59-5f1e-81a5-12cf064e3816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249107} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9d559c-a304-5add-bf80-843c7bcf1778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd399471-0d59-5f1e-81a5-12cf064e3816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249107} :answer-field/id #uuid "452d4c1f-6d4a-5321-8449-b83136ef15ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c98eb68-5485-54b6-b79b-f365d74db723" :question/math-academy-id "q-115631" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at  $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(8,-4),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(8) \\\\ 16 & =32p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417457e2-f527-590e-8a52-cfbea734e890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248006} {:answer/id #uuid "59f803eb-398e-554f-959f-407e6aa234ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248236} {:answer/id #uuid "772d4b5c-5e0b-5e8c-8d75-4a2c540051b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248474} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0eb5d3-7444-55f0-9242-f2645d848a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248973} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf395d3-b5c3-56fe-82d5-f51343d8cd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "772d4b5c-5e0b-5e8c-8d75-4a2c540051b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248474} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b8e70aa-72f7-5f54-9fef-39805521c959" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34ce73b1-4d1a-5e32-8244-a4c68ffbd052" :question/math-academy-id "q-115633" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-2,-2),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-2)^{2} & =4p(-2) \\\\ 4 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ccb079-926a-5d71-a4b5-2a3f7c343416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{8}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247629} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5ba719-5f1e-5166-b9d6-c5226048fe7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247835} {:answer/id #uuid "2f44c331-11c0-5df0-bbef-46c82af1a8ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247848} {:answer/id #uuid "928e0490-9198-5dea-9500-3e20cad763ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248723} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4e48dc-8a18-50cc-9d72-07b7205dd968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f44c331-11c0-5df0-bbef-46c82af1a8ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247848} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9464fef-08cc-5548-b814-d627646cdb2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b141f773-2517-5672-ab61-0399ba3f3a79" :question/math-academy-id "q-115636" :question/problem "![](images/57/5742f3f1161782d06b8a785d28d6c59b1e40aec37bd6b62268d6d41e1c662ee0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(-2,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-4,-4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-2,-1),$ we substitute $h=-2$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-4$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (-4+1)^{2} & =4p(-4+2) \\\\ 9 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{9}{8})(x+2) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{9}{8}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2674032e-cd4e-5e8a-803c-317914ccae04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247762} {:answer/id #uuid "839ff22d-c37d-5d91-9333-174668c3261a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248587} {:answer/id #uuid "ba27305a-d65b-5604-aaff-169ce339bb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249088} {:answer/id #uuid "c6443236-97c8-5e59-91fe-9a22a7cec2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=9(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249186} {:answer/id #uuid "dc878183-0dd0-5e61-ae12-26bab8ba8369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{12}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2674032e-cd4e-5e8a-803c-317914ccae04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247762} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f52642-018d-53e4-af4e-c4f058d871f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fff1310e-72c2-5e26-9574-a4434ee14fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-115642" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8ea1ad85f30978bed681f48e02f4af2f401a1f561390bee09683ead252fc1d7d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(1,-2)$ and passes through the point $(4,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-2),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (-3+2)^{2} & =4p(4-1) \\\\ 1 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{12})(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{12}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355fbc00-261f-53ee-8dcf-73fe6e42e797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247905} {:answer/id #uuid "4af1f11c-7e10-50c1-9a5b-ddcd30c9563e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248095} {:answer/id #uuid "58a5fee4-f000-5d2a-a869-da8133138d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248228} {:answer/id #uuid "9e115316-bacf-5821-b13a-ecd916aa4e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248827} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c1e0c0-f378-51f0-a377-0637fecf2b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "355fbc00-261f-53ee-8dcf-73fe6e42e797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247905} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cae7d88-3221-5c34-bd20-23e6289bab44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30a0f072-9dc6-5517-92da-25f253588788" :question/math-academy-id "q-115643" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f6330a6e9bc0e48326982c7b7509f3e2257de994c5736c813ede93a07491e70.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,-3)$ and passes through the point $(3,0).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(0,-3),$ we substitute $h=0$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(3,0),$ so we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (0+3)^{2} & =4p(3) \\\\ 9 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})x \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156c0dd5-6e39-5f04-a2cf-5d5dc02bd4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247617} {:answer/id #uuid "2fbb2f1f-caff-5f66-92f3-069a18a95d8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=36(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247854} {:answer/id #uuid "564d352c-519a-586d-9146-7140399b0611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248207} {:answer/id #uuid "724017f7-8d2c-566b-ad71-c51f4c355e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248421} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f76d45-8bcb-51a9-bc6f-283a0972d740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=-9(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "724017f7-8d2c-566b-ad71-c51f4c355e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248421} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c24b43-25a5-5bdf-b3ed-324954fa4e5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afe09fb4-eb0a-5d67-bf21-bab7a68c96cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-115646" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-3)$ and passes through the point $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-3),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(3,0),$ so we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (0+3)^{2} & =4p(3-1) \\\\ 9 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{8})(x-1) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189c7ea7-e51d-529e-81cd-d08955257660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{5}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247645} {:answer/id #uuid "5feb679b-21a6-5f8e-91a9-18154b4df9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{20}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248292} {:answer/id #uuid "6271c6bb-71be-51f3-aa98-5accc41c9ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{5}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248312} {:answer/id #uuid "8448f536-36df-5394-9b5c-7e6bb99e2ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{20}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248595} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf06e10-c6ad-5a47-8343-5d64e3bc9118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{5}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6271c6bb-71be-51f3-aa98-5accc41c9ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{5}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248312} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa908d94-7533-5961-8c30-b5936faa3381" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "909b1cd7-0685-5536-88b4-2736b32e6e6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115648" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,2)$ and passes through the point $(5,5).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(0,2),$ we substitute $h=0$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(5,5),$ so we can substitute $x=5$ and $y=5$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (5-2)^{2} & =4p(5) \\\\ 9 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{20})x \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{5}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34f7450d-9fe8-5185-b9b0-16c4a426024e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247898} {:answer/id #uuid "594fa969-2516-5e10-a314-0167ba360f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248232} {:answer/id #uuid "a4cba1a1-15d5-57d6-a679-1c36e6705554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248894} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ac8149-21da-5253-9b8b-314c31a75b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=2(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249199} {:answer/id #uuid "d720af45-ac2f-538e-a42c-0f8e90cb0ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d720af45-ac2f-538e-a42c-0f8e90cb0ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249328} :answer-field/id #uuid "05551464-0f03-5aa1-a716-e066278b117a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6a6caca-6d57-513d-aceb-bcb508f5faff" :question/math-academy-id "q-115649" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,0)$ and passes through the point $(0,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(2,0),$ we substitute $h=2$ and $k=0$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (-2)^{2} & =4p(0-2) \\\\ 4 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-2) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-2(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "423478f8-9aa2-5cda-afb3-17b256545aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248015} {:answer/id #uuid "4aee625f-94b6-586f-a54a-d0e72230fad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3x+1}-\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248094} {:answer/id #uuid "874ccc30-a28e-56ec-9671-fcc5053cb332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{4}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248634} {:answer/id #uuid "98869dd8-a1e3-512a-8c1e-3372b8c5a8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248785} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a83092-cd3f-5932-8afb-77fe5a6631f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{1}{3x+1}+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "423478f8-9aa2-5cda-afb3-17b256545aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248015} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cafb8c1-951c-5e34-ba7e-1900668fa6ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "031d8c13-fb73-52e6-afed-79556c97e9a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115656" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{3x+1}+\\frac{B}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(3x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+1=A(3x+1)^{2}+B(3x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+1 & =A{(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+1)}^{2}+B(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+1)+C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(0)^{2}+1 & =A(3(0)+1)^{2}+B(3(0)+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =A+B+2 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(-1)^{2}+1 & =A(3(-1)+1)^{2}+B(3(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 10 & =4A-2B+2 \\\\ 4A-2B & =8 \\\\ 2A-B & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $2A-B=4$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =4-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{3x+1}-\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+1}-\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54d923c2-b2f8-5d1c-a1fc-68a056f22c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248187} {:answer/id #uuid "990bfcfa-1f99-553c-a8ba-07aa7350fb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248791} {:answer/id #uuid "b521e98f-6c0a-5d30-b8c2-17d7c4a8510a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249044} {:answer/id #uuid "cf02c307-1d50-5567-ac85-0f7d14bc9b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249261} {:answer/id #uuid "dece391e-5beb-53bd-9d1e-fa41c6cd1e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b521e98f-6c0a-5d30-b8c2-17d7c4a8510a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249044} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8347731-1849-5e44-ab0d-b77e59a7eb41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b1b65b0-769f-5dc5-a09c-d74cd95adc54" :question/math-academy-id "q-115665" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-2 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+5(0)-2 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+5) \\\\ -2 & =B-5 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x+3}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+5=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d86c2f6-a314-54ab-8dcf-d3faec6a6acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247547} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf83c5f-777a-5606-b25b-ad1a2d27450f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248387} {:answer/id #uuid "884fc272-292f-5726-b2b3-9221568754c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248642} {:answer/id #uuid "bad95a73-618f-506a-a148-60bbb75b007b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249092} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d383ca-dc7c-5b9d-8c65-750c36a50197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d383ca-dc7c-5b9d-8c65-750c36a50197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249579} :answer-field/id #uuid "348ab176-46a8-53a5-a44b-b51495ebb88c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fcac2c1-8de9-5c0d-89db-ccf7066e1090" :question/math-academy-id "q-115667" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{D}{x+2},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $D.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+20 & =(x-2)(x+2)+D({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+20 & =(0-2)(0+2)+D({0}^{2}+8) \\\\ 20 & =-4+8D \\\\ 24 & =8D \\\\ D & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{3}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+8=u.$ Then, $x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\text{d}x=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf1b20f-3f1e-5d39-b833-924bd37ace5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10})+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247541} {:answer/id #uuid "1778ff91-6f16-5705-8283-db705faace3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247636} {:answer/id #uuid "30031e73-eda6-5628-a936-a4c56fda636b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247857} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0be9ab-d229-5515-9f4f-676bfd0e8f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249222} {:answer/id #uuid "e64b227d-d62e-52ef-8489-7acf21e9c49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1778ff91-6f16-5705-8283-db705faace3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247636} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f2bda8-8928-56d1-a14f-bbe51de1a1c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa383850-ed95-5745-b056-448c57db2176" :question/math-academy-id "q-115673" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x-27 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-5(0)-27 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+20) \\\\ -27 & =B-20 \\\\ B & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-7}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+20=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d004b1-8a79-5237-9a43-c5d695947628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247474} {:answer/id #uuid "73d79229-ca1a-597f-bc97-2007a1959580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248441} {:answer/id #uuid "859ad402-4287-5864-9a3a-b27433df0b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248615} {:answer/id #uuid "d816ae40-b699-5487-b9be-edd17a53da31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249333} {:answer/id #uuid "fbddd6e4-1678-53af-9fac-a34da478aee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "859ad402-4287-5864-9a3a-b27433df0b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248615} :answer-field/id #uuid "39da731f-4eac-5eb0-8967-592e81989b3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54337009-4b4f-594c-8090-7c81fe847987" :question/math-academy-id "q-115686" :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+1$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{C}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+1)(x-4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-9x+5 & =(Ax+B)(x-4)+C({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047c28c1-7366-514f-8a71-70b8574e8b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247456} {:answer/id #uuid "10cab1b6-0eef-57ac-9c3c-e79de55b6805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247573} {:answer/id #uuid "567e1f65-c305-5e5b-8580-4126bb121c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248209} {:answer/id #uuid "aef9856b-bea1-549a-9bcd-ac2c1039a2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248991} {:answer/id #uuid "d72b33b4-9d9a-5a6a-bd0d-01f35633bd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d72b33b4-9d9a-5a6a-bd0d-01f35633bd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249329} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7d57d04-325f-59f4-bb1d-89f0fcd9bb08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "412d22df-d958-56dd-8884-22116d9638ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-115690" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+6$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+6}+\\frac{C}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+6)(x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x & =(Ax+B)(x-3)+C({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=-4,$ and $C=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+6| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a06faf40-267a-585a-a698-8d1c4a8418b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248857} {:answer/id #uuid "b11b9a13-576c-58f2-b1fa-068e66f1591c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249010} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c1daa1-28ba-5945-bf62-2d01df1e7ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249162} {:answer/id #uuid "e31429d4-0443-599a-a3a4-365a3f4492c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249430} {:answer/id #uuid "ebec7c76-3253-5217-8050-0e4ee5b66e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebec7c76-3253-5217-8050-0e4ee5b66e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249496} :answer-field/id #uuid "4adf0cfe-17c4-56d0-b154-4a4709b2b3f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b36c8e07-4439-57ae-b9d1-276ef485c6ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-115693" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+3$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+3)(x+4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+5x-9 & =(Ax+B)(x+4)+C({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-3,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+3| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e0f5cbb-98e8-55aa-b890-984b305ed4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248530} {:answer/id #uuid "94947933-b100-53c7-8fdb-0b6d8803da1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248745} {:answer/id #uuid "95219ef7-6026-599f-b799-3fe1f3c2a17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248749} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9d1e02-75e2-51b5-87f3-47ea9e6004dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249502} {:answer/id #uuid "f80a858d-c201-59de-aa39-33d3a1e7d443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec9d1e02-75e2-51b5-87f3-47ea9e6004dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249502} :answer-field/id #uuid "d433bef3-6036-5aac-9df3-3dc1353dab2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "74feace7-d730-58bc-b03e-4652e0e5c170" :question/math-academy-id "q-115710" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  $\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD=(4)^{2}-4(1)(-6)=40\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{40}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x({x}^{2}+4x-6),$ and we get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+12x-12=A({x}^{2}+4x-6)+(Bx+C)x\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=2$, and $C=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}=\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+4x-6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(2x+4)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ddb4ed-90f0-5df5-b21a-41d53d8fe661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}{3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248021} {:answer/id #uuid "49d41c54-3f94-52cd-a66b-4ba669efe7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248079} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f4069d-2ea6-59d1-acc4-c6d742ba2c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249043} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7d86ae-66d8-5482-ac29-aa13eed6597e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249254} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c6e3ba-2427-5970-9188-62ee0b23275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1c6e3ba-2427-5970-9188-62ee0b23275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249284} :answer-field/id #uuid "6905e022-77c6-597f-b4cb-140b5c642224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d0e590b-4ba3-5c1f-9a3a-31d9c817ed33" :question/math-academy-id "q-115712" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  $\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD=(5)^{2}-4(2)(-6)=73\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{73}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6),$ and we get\n\n$$\n12-5x=A(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)+(Bx+C)x\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-2,B=4$, and $C=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}=-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u=2{x}^{2}+5x-6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(4x+5)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2e5bd3-f473-510b-9434-aa7443cb05b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247670} {:answer/id #uuid "1d717368-1df9-5a63-8977-b45159997003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247687} {:answer/id #uuid "89616672-693d-59fb-88ad-7b1432546374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248644} {:answer/id #uuid "a9969d54-7454-5477-b1ca-0b4165edbcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248931} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ccf359-d194-5c0f-99a1-734608e48ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d717368-1df9-5a63-8977-b45159997003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247687} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d962fb6-9b43-5d0e-8fc2-499dbcc92713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "734835be-6e59-529d-977d-cd321f0badd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-115772" :question/problem "Given that $r=\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27edf7e2-1949-5b19-8951-ef3c63f01da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247781} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea79f41-fe25-516b-bca1-bc3026a8967b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247839} {:answer/id #uuid "8417dd2f-c817-521f-8b3a-8a613dc15c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248592} {:answer/id #uuid "a49939f7-f7ec-5933-b7fb-eda0850f82be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248891} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd0cdbd-93c2-547d-a725-3c921538ee20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8417dd2f-c817-521f-8b3a-8a613dc15c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248592} :answer-field/id #uuid "df247728-6fc4-5d6e-ad05-31984fda9cc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b468347a-5af4-5e0d-add1-8cb76b34a17b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115776" :question/problem "Given that $r=-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=12$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=12$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})) \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{16} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aacad592-c837-5a68-8f8b-57c3d7dcb43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248943} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d09c12-1f86-5e78-8465-e8c7d48b9e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249017} {:answer/id #uuid "b2637471-8d8a-5bbe-bd12-5b87dfc106b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249023} {:answer/id #uuid "d0726f1e-da5c-51b1-9a97-44846fb7928e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249271} {:answer/id #uuid "f632b040-af98-517b-9654-3f33648e9c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2637471-8d8a-5bbe-bd12-5b87dfc106b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249023} :answer-field/id #uuid "02e92776-40a0-56fe-8e7e-05efd3ee58e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b056bc9-1b86-5285-9289-f5246eedec51" :question/math-academy-id "q-115788" :question/problem "Solve for $m$ where $\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}=\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $m=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by ${m}^{3},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}} & =\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}} \\\\ {m}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}) & ={m}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}) \\\\ {m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+{m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}} & ={m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}} \\\\ \\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{3m-1}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}}+\\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}} & =\\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2m+3}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}} \\\\ 3m-1+m+2 & =2m+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3m-1+m+2 & =2m+3 \\\\ 4m+1 & =2m+3 \\\\ 4m-2m & =3-1 \\\\ 2m & =2 \\\\ m & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=1\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b0fb4a6-a0a8-5f73-8a19-abd9ab8d7b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247947} {:answer/id #uuid "512c886f-c605-5743-8740-b7c0b7952acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248153} {:answer/id #uuid "a792d170-bee0-5203-810d-3149d20c6d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248914} {:answer/id #uuid "b68ef072-78e4-53b5-8670-2baeaf867b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249060} {:answer/id #uuid "b69e7dcd-1896-5e5e-b67d-4d01a47dee7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a792d170-bee0-5203-810d-3149d20c6d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248914} :answer-field/id #uuid "87bed9b6-d71c-5c36-af6d-4aec4c0b6168" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb3909a1-5d95-5bcb-832e-e8e09e292f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-115791" :question/problem "Solve for $z$ where $\\frac{z+1}{2z}=2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $z=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $2z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ 2z\\cdot (\\frac{z+1}{2z}) & =2z\\cdot (2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z}) \\\\ 2z\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2z\\cdot 2-2z\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ \\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} & =2z\\cdot 2-\\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ z+1 & =4z-(2z+1) \\\\ z+1 & =4z-2z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =4z-2z-1 \\\\ z+1 & =2z-1 \\\\ -z & =-2 \\\\ z & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db9a03b-7189-51c6-beb4-58184628399a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247551} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffff95f-e0da-51b7-b2b1-60ad819c5e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247856} {:answer/id #uuid "5a100c5a-10c6-507e-ab3d-717c80187730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248239} {:answer/id #uuid "de207cc8-0ebf-5357-9d93-bba635b2e97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249392} {:answer/id #uuid "fc55d1bf-5b4e-5900-8dba-dfc04d82fcc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc55d1bf-5b4e-5900-8dba-dfc04d82fcc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249625} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c87277-896d-50dd-82b5-385c9dad90a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c001b720-cfa8-54e2-8c25-eb8cf8283524" :question/math-academy-id "q-115792" :question/problem "Solve for $w$ where $1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w}=\\frac{w+5}{3w}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $w=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $3w,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w} & =\\frac{w+5}{3w} \\\\ 3w\\cdot (1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w}) & =3w\\cdot (\\frac{w+5}{3w}) \\\\ 3w\\cdot 1-3w\\cdot \\frac{2w-3}{3w} & =3w\\cdot \\frac{w+5}{3w} \\\\ 3w-\\cancel{3w}\\cdot \\frac{2w-3}{\\cancel{3w}} & =\\cancel{3w}\\cdot \\frac{w+5}{\\cancel{3w}} \\\\ 3w-(2w-3) & =w+5 \\\\ 3w-2w+3 & =w+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $w,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w-2w+3 & =w+5 \\\\ w+3 & =w+5 \\\\ w-w & =5-3 \\\\ 0 & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a false statement. Therefore, we conclude that there is no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbe6c94-b151-5c1d-9c24-6ac6eaedff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247527} {:answer/id #uuid "284a2d2d-aec2-56b2-a83a-447fe88b345e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247782} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd2d177-bb80-5fbb-b4fb-239d54aa4023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248101} {:answer/id #uuid "9a88c5c8-97bc-5272-bbaa-7a0f74513dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248802} {:answer/id #uuid "bfeb1cf3-0a3b-5e0b-81f5-f88d8973a54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bbe6c94-b151-5c1d-9c24-6ac6eaedff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247527} :answer-field/id #uuid "929d68dd-7d98-50ea-b0e6-bb084af9ebee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a05ffdc-3b52-5cee-9869-369fd44c61d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115794" :question/problem "Which value of $z$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $z=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3z$ and $6z.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3z,6z)=6z.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z} \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z})+6z\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ \\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3z}\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{\\cancel{3z}})+\\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) \\\\ 2z-1 & =2\\cdot (3z-1)+z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-1 & =2(3z-1)+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =6z-2+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =7z-3 \\\\ 2z-7z & =-3+1 \\\\ -5z & =-2 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=\\frac{2}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096c553b-7828-57f6-b436-0ebba6db180e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247506} {:answer/id #uuid "3662123b-4884-519f-a2c9-aa488f53fa7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247914} {:answer/id #uuid "92fdc3b4-daad-5ee8-aaeb-bc28ccc8412a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248727} {:answer/id #uuid "9af98b09-2493-587d-aaf7-02ccdebddcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248804} {:answer/id #uuid "d40225e2-a3b2-5686-908b-b61ab91cd370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9af98b09-2493-587d-aaf7-02ccdebddcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248804} :answer-field/id #uuid "144839cb-882f-5a1a-a1fa-8d94766d1746" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a80168e-4de0-55ab-b42f-115076b5300e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115898" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=6y?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(\\frac{3}{2})y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65fe4c0d-ace7-531e-beb7-ee58e3de3451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248342} {:answer/id #uuid "74587b67-e4e7-5697-a5a6-6030edcf2fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248449} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f72a67-c47a-5be9-ad72-37a4d337a419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248933} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc96723-add8-5dc4-9b11-f81c31894208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249379} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc93ce4-82f8-54be-85e4-eb320b4e4a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcc93ce4-82f8-54be-85e4-eb320b4e4a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249634} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b1ce8f9-82c3-51dc-9505-eea5dfb49165" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b53a4d3-8dde-5b34-8af3-3a4b04e9f45b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115905" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=2x?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4(\\frac{1}{2})x.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106bf08a-6288-5161-b589-b3a6bfd5d596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247568} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecf6f9d-a2f5-586a-b320-3685a62fe9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248538} {:answer/id #uuid "9d436ef0-528d-5c42-acfd-fecb45ade3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248822} {:answer/id #uuid "e75adbb9-dff5-55d0-b063-3054aec88c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249463} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0d9b69-2f3f-545f-8357-505ac2a681cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d436ef0-528d-5c42-acfd-fecb45ade3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248822} :answer-field/id #uuid "791f4c31-853e-56a1-9a6c-8d9759f6db39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ec04397-a1d2-5a43-be90-9c3301fd7dc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115911" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y-1)^{2}=5(x+7)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4(\\frac{5}{4})(x+7).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-7$ and $k=1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-7,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0918bb01-088a-5fb8-9976-6affbd0c602e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247502} {:answer/id #uuid "54e90a0d-89ad-5674-86ec-1963b16305e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248189} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6bd378-5100-5d78-bfdc-40d8310a4f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248286} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a03cc9-862b-55ef-af02-5e36a687bb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248869} {:answer/id #uuid "d19f41ed-1b0f-5895-92ef-f2629810f757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f6bd378-5100-5d78-bfdc-40d8310a4f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248286} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c21528-e209-5727-b3ee-515c255eaaf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27f3a217-c646-5edd-93b0-a8b079b9f019" :question/math-academy-id "q-115919" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to three-fifths of the rate of increase of the diameter. Determine the radius of the circle at that moment." :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{D}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0237b559-7d33-5cd0-9da8-8d8b205f4742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247438} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb99e31-a544-5220-80a8-c1892b899c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247705} {:answer/id #uuid "cac77d12-ae34-5ddd-8afb-806b1d42068b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249230} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d99394-c6b7-5118-9b32-40e3e577b39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249294} {:answer/id #uuid "fc368912-431d-5c1c-94c8-d1e252061d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2d99394-c6b7-5118-9b32-40e3e577b39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249294} :answer-field/id #uuid "05de44ca-2588-5951-97b7-06f8b0b05597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f403d57-f6d2-5901-9ad6-4def1edb7127" :question/math-academy-id "q-116104" :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x$ and ${x}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,{x}^{2})={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${x}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x})-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) & =x\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\cancel{x}})-\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) \\\\ (3x-1) & =x\\cdot 2-(x-3) \\\\ (3x-1) & =2x-(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-1) & =2x-(x-3) \\\\ 3x-1 & =2x-x+3 \\\\ 3x-1 & =x+3 \\\\ 3x-x & =3+1 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095b621d-d28c-5438-ac86-bb537204c4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247505} {:answer/id #uuid "19938bf6-8fa9-534a-99f3-a34885b9ec72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247656} {:answer/id #uuid "4194167a-a248-589b-8287-fdb853c46d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248008} {:answer/id #uuid "f3500a3c-8d1a-531b-a225-4a4c4972bd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249554} {:answer/id #uuid "f647ed95-e4d5-5ab0-baa1-dad4fc8d3414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3500a3c-8d1a-531b-a225-4a4c4972bd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249554} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bf22087-6398-52e3-9c0b-1effe99dbc91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3783baa6-61fe-5e5f-a6e5-bd47f3137af5" :question/math-academy-id "q-116106" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2{m}^{2},$ ${m}^{2}$ and $m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m,{m}^{2},2{m}^{2})=2{m}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2{m}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}} \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}) \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m})-2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}) \\\\ 2m\\cdot \\cancel{m}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{m}})-\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{3m+4}{\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{{m}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{m}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2m\\cdot 3-(3m+4) & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6m-(3m+4) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6m-(3m+4) & =2 \\\\ 6m-3m-4 & =2 \\\\ 3m & =6 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7b94ae-5c5d-5a90-9f9b-48dff29de3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250659} {:answer/id #uuid "4a18df07-f7d9-59d3-8f70-e9b3b6738591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251169} {:answer/id #uuid "8e36fe03-f4cf-58d9-97e7-5ee23757234d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251756} {:answer/id #uuid "91f5f78b-03ee-59f5-849c-c8de47775dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251801} {:answer/id #uuid "f469076d-5ab7-5e45-85bf-58ca2c786c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a18df07-f7d9-59d3-8f70-e9b3b6738591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251169} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24ab2b9-6b03-53c1-a30b-f4718e0e1f4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10207108-a210-580a-9bc1-6306b708682a" :question/math-academy-id "q-116650" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4fa141d5af6f23e2eaa1d416def8f84e21e48f775b5b195cbc8ed74e4bb64218.png)\n\nA circular iceberg is melting in such a way that the superficial circle is decreasing its area at a rate of $24\\pi \\,{\\text{km}}^{2}\\text{/year}.$ Calculate the rate at which the circumference of the iceberg is decreasing at the moment when the radius is  $6\\,\\text{km}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=6,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=6\\,\\text{km},$ $C=2\\pi (6)=12\\pi \\text{ km}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi $ and $C=12\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{12\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{24\\pi }{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\text{ km/year}.$"}]
